// SL-316 — a CLOSURE BODY that really suspends, written in an IMPORTED module's
// helper, is refused AT THE CLOSURE.
//
// This is the cell SL-316 found, and the silent one. Its entry-module twin is
// refused WHENEVER SOMETHING IN THE PROGRAM IS FRAMED — a suspension written
// directly in a closure body lands on the enclosing function's own effect node,
// so a frame IS built and the driven caller's own body scan refuses it. (With
// no root at all neither twin was refused; that pair is
// `coro_closure_body_suspends_with_no_driven_root.saw` and its imported
// sibling.) The imported twin was not refused here either: the enclosing
// helper carries only the conservative closure-call cause, no frame is built for
// it, NO FRAME BUILDER EVER SCANS ITS BODY, and the `yield_now()` lowered
// outside every frame, where it codegens to nothing. The program compiled and
// printed 0 drive steps where two were owed. Same program shape, one file
// boundary apart, two outcomes — and the silent one is the third outcome designs
// 96/101/104 rule out.
//
// Design 275 U2 asks the question of the CLOSURE rather than of its caller, so
// the answer no longer depends on who happens to reach it. The refusal is
// anchored at the closure, inside the dependency, because that is the code that
// cannot work.
//
// EXPECT: error
// COMPILE-FLAGS: --module-path sl316closure={TESTDIR}/../modules/sl316closure
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: this closure body suspends
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: a closure body is not driven
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: lib.saw:23:17

import std.task.*
import sl316closure.{each_yield_public}

func through_dep(values: &Vector<Int>) -> Int {
    each_yield_public(&values)
}

func main() {
    let values: Vector<Int> = [1, 2]
    print(__saw_drive_steps(through_dep(&values)))
}
